	I. Descriptive Statistics :
	-  describe() function
	A. Measure of central tendency :
	Mean -
	
	
	 Median -  Mid value
	
	
	 Mode -  Eg : 1,2,3,3 --- Mode = 3 , High frequency
	
	
	B. Measure of Asymmetry :
	 Skewness -  To check whether the data is located  left side or right side.
	
	
	- Mean > Median and Mode == Positive Skew == Data Left side == Outlier is Right side
	- Mean == Median == Mode == Zero Skew  ( Always consider this) -- Normal Distribution
	- Mode> Median > Mean == Negative Skew == Data right side == Outlier is left side
	- 
	- We can solve multiple data skewness using SCIPY Library or STATS API
	
	
	Kurtosis - Skewness and Kurtosis have same meaning , but graphs are different
	
	
	
	+ve Kurtosis ==  Positive Skew == Leptokurtic
	-ve Kurtosis == Negative Skew == Platykurtic 
	0 Kurtosis == Zero Skew ==  Mesokurtic
	
	
	C. Measure of Variability : 
	
	 Variance  -  Spread of Data around mean
	
	
	Mew == Population mean
	X bar == Sample mean
	N = Number Total Population
	 n = Number Sample population total
	
	standard deviation - 
	
	
	Coefficient of variation - 
	
	- Does not have a unit of measurement 
	- Universal across datasets
	- Perfect for comparisons  
	
	
	
	---- 
	
		II. Inferential Statistics :
	
	A. Probability and Distribution
	
	Rolling 1 dice --> Uniform Distribution
	Toss coin --> Uniform Distibution
	
	
	


	- Every time we need to consider normal distribution 

	B. Standardization  and Z score 
	- It is one of the important technique which will implement in machine Learning, 
	- Standardization == Z score == ML (feature scaling technique)
	- In time series == White noise








	C. Central limit theorem 

	- In a dataset , we randomly pull some sample records and mean them x1
	- From another sample we mean them x2 , x3,…..xn 
	- Sample should be greater than number of records is called central limit theorem

Eg :  There are 960 records of students range from 1 to 1000
We sample 13 samples -- each sample 25 records 

	D. Standard error 

Sigma(SD) / root n 





	E. Confidence Interval

Test case 1:
I am 95% confident I score between 80 -90 
80-90 is  == estimator 
(80+90)/2 is  == estimate
	- 80 is lower bound 
	- 90 is upper bound

Test case 2:
I am 99% confident I score between 90-98

Result = 86
95% confident test case wins

Population CI 
Sample CI 



Population variance know == Z test (Helps finding confidence interval) -- Used for sample
Sample variance unknow == T test

Confidence level. = 1- alpha
Alpha = Error


Point estimate == Mean 
Standard error == Sigma(SD) / root n 

Eg : 


Mean = 100,200
Std = 15,000




We use Z table(statistical table to compute Z test) for Z test  ---- 






P value == 95% confidence means --- 5% error (0.05)
P value always calculate as 0.05

T - Test (For Populations)





T test invervals


Z Test intervals

	- In ML algorithms we only use T test 
	

	

